{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The proof may only establish compactness for L-infinity equivalence classes rather than admissible Borel policies. Null sets and measurable representatives may not align across theta. The reduction may also hide assumptions about private randomness and delayed feedback.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurability audit","critic stress test"],"summary":"Write a referee-ready proof of the fixed finite T, finite grid G reward-measure domination theorem. Show any private-randomized public-price policy has the same expected rewards as prefix kernels q_t(P_1:t), derive L_theta(q) from payoff-weighted measures, prove weak-star compactness of the feasible kernel set, and conclude product compactness/no-gap.","title":"Close The No-Gap Proof","why_promising":"This is the main conditional gap in the current solver attempt. If it works, the finite-class Bayes value theorem extends cleanly to dominated infinite classes."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Actual market reward sets may be too structured to realize the abstract finite-support pathology. Countable or Borel-identifiable versions may be easy for universal policies, collapsing the obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","model realization","measurability audit"],"summary":"Search for a natural public-exogenous infinite class where every finite subclass has small grid minimax value but the full class has positive or linear minimax value. Avoid relying on non-Borel labels if possible; otherwise prove that any standard-Borel attempt must fail under identifiable or dominated assumptions.","title":"Natural Infinite-Class Gap","why_promising":"It would clarify whether product closedness is a real mathematical assumption or mostly a technical artifact of the current proof."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Uniform approximation over all policies may be essentially total-variation strength and therefore too restrictive. Comparator suprema may amplify small measure errors by order T.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","constant tracking","counterexample search"],"summary":"Prove an approximate no-gap theorem: if each reward measure and grid comparator value is uniformly approximated by a dominated surrogate game within epsilon_T, bound the true full-class grid value by the finite-subclass Bayes modulus plus an explicit constant times epsilon_T. Identify the weakest useful metric on the payoff-weighted measures.","title":"Approximate Domination","why_promising":"This could turn the exact domination theorem into a robust tool for realistic infinite classes and finite approximations."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Measurable decoder and argmax assumptions may be nontrivial. If decode time grows with T, early regret may dominate. Partial or noisy decoding likely falls back to the Bayes-gap framework.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","measurable selection","examples"],"summary":"Formalize an orthogonal theorem for nondominated classes where a public prefix decodes theta after m steps. Under standard Borel and measurable oracle assumptions, bound regret by the pre-decode loss plus O(T/K), or compute the exact pre-decode Bayes gap.","title":"Identifiable Singular Classes","why_promising":"Common domination excludes many deterministic Dirac-like classes that are intuitively easy once the parameter is publicly revealed."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best fixed quote in expectation may collapse to the Bayes action. Future spikes can trade old quotes and couple blocks. Hidden bits may reveal too much, and the construction may violate the target tail envelope.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound construction","Bayes-gap computation","counterexample search"],"summary":"Use the finite-grid Psi_G formula to design horizon-indexed finite classes satisfying a tail envelope B(h), especially B(h)=h^{-alpha}, with accumulated Bayes gap near the truncation upper rate. Explore Assouad or Fano-style hidden bits across scales, limited-leakage terminal observations, and quote intervals that prevent one reveal from solving all blocks.","title":"Multi-Scale Tail Lower Bounds","why_promising":"Matching power-tail lower rates are the largest open quantitative gap; the known one-block qD atom is too weak."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The resulting quantity may be tautological, essentially restating the minimax value. Infinite-class product-closure issues would still remain, and the modulus may be hard to estimate.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","upper-lower comparison","toy examples"],"summary":"Define and test a tail-like predictability modulus based on full versus H-truncated least-favorable Bayes value, such as the gap between full and truncated Psi_G over priors or between full and truncated minimax values. Compare it to delta_T(H), Delta_T(H), and raw beta(H) on the known examples.","title":"Bayes-Truncation Modulus","why_promising":"It may bridge the accepted truncation upper theorem with the minimax/Bayes characterization while avoiding false raw delay tails."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"A randomized mixture or doubling-style schedule may avoid the proposed lower bound. Later blocks may contaminate horizon calculations. This is separate from the horizon-known model if that is the final scope.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search","model taxonomy"],"summary":"Resolve the horizon-free known-law regime. Formalize the super-growing two-deadline deterministic sequence stress test and prove that any single policy knowing the infinite sequence but not T has linear regret on a subsequence, or find an anytime policy that defeats the construction.","title":"Anytime Known-Law Boundary","why_promising":"The known-law oracle is horizon-aware; this determines whether fixed-process learnability stays trivial without horizon knowledge."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Several causal regret notions are plausible, so the work may become model design rather than solving the exogenous problem. The result may not inform the main theorem if action-dependent prices are declared out of scope.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","counterexample search","causal semantics"],"summary":"Write a minimal action-dependent price protocol and either construct a counterexample to the delayed full-information truncation reduction or prove an analogue under strong nonanticipativity. Explicitly define whether comparator rewards use learner-induced or comparator-induced price paths.","title":"Action-Dependent Audit","why_promising":"All accepted upper and value-characterization results require public exogenous prices; this closes a major quantifier ambiguity in the original problem."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
